Wire member type exprs through algebra/

`walk_if_atom`'s `IfAtom::Var` branch was previously skipping member
type annotations: `resolve_type_expr` returned `None` for
`TypeExpr::Member`, so the annotated el ended up with no concrete
type and the close pass had nothing to propagate. Now
`walk_var_type_expr` resolves all three type-expr kinds, and for
`e.Name` it walks `e`, looks up `Name` in `e`'s model body via
`member_scope_and_parents`, and builds a `ConcreteType` whose parent
chain is `e`'s parents with `e` itself appended.

The var-if-atom site is reworked to be re-entrant: el allocation and
`semantic_els` insertion stay idempotent, and the concrete type is
recorded only once it resolves. A member annotation whose parent's
type is not yet known leaves the el untyped on this pass; a later
iteration retries after close has propagated more typing information.

A side effect is that the algebra side now detects the conflict in
`bad-dep-func-result-type` (where an explicit `s_1.El` annotation
clashes with an `s_0.El` constraint imposed by a func-arg). Because
algebra-side errors come before eqlog-side errors in the iterator
chain in `build.rs`, the algebra rendering wins on ties; its type
ordering is driven by close-pass timing rather than source order. The
expected output is updated accordingly.
